Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A velveteen rabbit sits wedged in the top of a Christmas stocking, a sprig of holly between his paws. His coat is spotted brown and white, his ears lined with pink sateen. Other toys spill from the stocking: a toy engine, a clockwork mouse, nuts and oranges.

The BOY, six years old, pulls the RABBIT free and hugs him tight for two hours.

Aunts and Uncles arrive. Parcels are unwrapped. The Rabbit is set aside on the floor and forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The Rabbit lives in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor. Mechanical toys look down on him. The model boat boasts of his rigging. Timothy the wooden lion pretends he is connected with Government.

The SKIN HORSE lies near the fender, brown coat bald in patches.

The Rabbit and the Skin Horse lie side by side.

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NANA searches for the china dog. She sees the open toy cupboard, grabs the Rabbit by one ear and thrusts him into the Boy's arms.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

The Boy hugs the Rabbit tight. The Rabbit is pushed under the pillow, then pulled out again. The Boy talks to him in whispers and makes tunnels under the bedclothes.

The Rabbit snuggles under the Boy's chin and dreams.

Time passes. The Rabbit's fur grows shabbier. His tail becomes unsewn. The pink rubs off his nose.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

The Boy pushes the Rabbit in a wheelbarrow. They picnic on the grass. The Boy builds fairy huts under the raspberry canes.

The Boy is called away. The Rabbit lies on the lawn until dusk. Nana finds him with a candle, wet with dew and earthy.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

The Boy sits up in bed.

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit hears and knows the nursery magic has happened. He is Real.

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

The Boy makes the Rabbit a nest in the bracken and wanders off. Two wild rabbits creep close, noses twitching.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The wild rabbit hops on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit sits still. He has no hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

The wild rabbits laugh and dance away.

RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Boy returns and carries the Rabbit home.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy lies flushed and feverish. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, afraid they will take him away.

The fever turns. The Boy recovers.

The doctor and Nana stand at the window.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

EXT. END OF GARDEN - NIGHT

The Rabbit lies in a sack with old picture-books behind the fowl-house. He wriggles his head free and looks out at the raspberry thicket. A tear falls from his shabby velvet nose.

A mysterious flower grows where the tear fell. Its blossom opens. The FAIRY steps out, dressed in pearl and dew-drops.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am? I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She gathers him up and flies into the wood.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

The Fairy sets the Rabbit on the grass among the wild rabbits.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits still, remembering he has no hind legs. Then something tickles his nose. He lifts a hind toe to scratch it. He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny. Long whiskers. He leaps and dances with the others.

The Fairy is gone.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

The Boy plays among the trees. Two rabbits creep from the bracken. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted.

The Boy watches them.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit looks back at the Boy, then hops away with the other into the bracken.
